<section id="about" class="part">
<h2>What this page is</h2>
<p>The <code>fortress-repo</code> skill holds the rules that bind every worker in the repository. This page shows you each change that landed in it, as a diff with its reason, so that you can check what the workers are now told whenever you want. To reverse a change, send a comment to Claude on its item, or say so in chat.</p>
</section>

<section id="s1" class="part">
<h2>Not yet read</h2>
<p>Fifteen items, all new. The page holds only what you have not read. At 17:06 UTC on 5 November, you said that you had read the page up to change 17, so the new items are numbered on from 18.</p>
<p>Climb batch 26 landed on <code>main</code>, and its landing made some sentences of the skill false. The record-landing workflow then updated the skill in three commits, after midnight UTC on 6 November. Changes 18 to 24 are the orientation update of <code>revival-changes.md</code>: the orientation workflow rewrote that part for the landing, its judge said to install every changed section, and the skill writer copied them in. Changes 25 to 32 bring <code>interpreter.md</code> and <code>compiler.md</code> in line with what the batch changed in walk and in the code generator. A cold read of these changes then found passages that would send a worker wrong or make it search, and the skill writer fixed them. Each item shows the net change of both rounds.</p>
<p>Each item shows the text on <code>main</code> now, against the text before the first of the three commits. The words that changed are underlined, and the words that went are struck through.</p>

<section id="l18" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 18 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;</span>Why walk refuses overloads on two instantiations</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;, line 28</p>
<p><strong>The compiled checker applies the exclusion rule (P-43, P-97).</strong> It refuses a type that extends two instantiations of one generic trait. Walk <span class="cut">applies no instantiation exclusion, so it</span> <span class="add">does not apply the rule: it</span> runs such a type, and refuses overloads on two instantiations, as <span class="cut">at <code>Tg[\3\]</code> and <code>Tg[\4\]</code></span> <span class="add"><code>g(b: Tg[\3\])</code> beside <code>g(b: Tg[\4\])</code>, because it does not read them as excluding</span> (row 416). The specification marks the Working Draft's examples that break the rule &ldquo;Not allowed&rdquo;: do not copy them.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The sentence used &ldquo;exclusion&rdquo; in two senses: the rule that no type extends two instantiations of one trait, and two parameter types that exclude each other. A reader could take it that walk refuses such overloads on purpose. The text now says that walk does not apply the rule, and that it refuses the two overloads because it does not read their types as excluding each other. The cold read then had the example written as two overloads, as <code>interpreter.md</code> writes it, in place of two bare types.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l19" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 19 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;</span>An example for the second kind of self-typed trait</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;, line 39, the second condition of a self-typed trait</p>
<ul><li><code>T</code> has a parameter <code>X</code> bounded by <code>T</code> itself, as <code>trait T[\X extends T[\X\]\]</code> (P-146), or by a self-typed trait <span class="add"><code>U[\X\]</code></span> that <code>T</code> <span class="cut">extends at <code>X</code>.</span> <span class="add">extends, as <code>trait T[\X extends U[\X\]\] extends U[\X\]</code>.</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> &ldquo;A self-typed trait that <code>T</code> extends at <code>X</code>&rdquo; read two ways: <code>X</code> bounded by <code>U[\X\]</code> where <code>T</code> extends <code>U[\X\]</code>, or <code>T</code> extending <code>U</code> at some other argument. The text now names the trait <code>U[\X\]</code> and gives the first reading as an example, which is the form that walk's code describes.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l20" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 20 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;</span>Walk now loads the self-typed pair in a program's own code</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;, lines 43 to 46</p>
<p>When the checker applies the overloading rules to a method of <code>T</code>, <code>self</code> has the type <code>T[\X\]</code>, except in its test of whether two domains exclude each other. There <code>self</code> has the intersection type of <code>T[\X\]</code> and <code>X</code> (P-157). With this reading and the bounds that the test reads (&ldquo;Dispatch and overloading&rdquo;), the library's generic <code>+</code> and <code>-</code> between an array and a number are valid beside <code>AdditiveGroup</code>'s own. <span class="cut">Walk loads the library's pairs, but refuses such a pair where a program's own component declares both (row 804).</span> <span class="add">Walk reads <code>self</code> in the same way, so it loads such a pair in the library and in a program's own component. Walk's load check, which is not the verdict (&ldquo;Dispatch and overloading&rdquo;), still refuses the pair in two cases:</span></p>
<ul>
<li><span class="add"><code>T</code> is self-typed only through another self-typed trait that it extends;</span></li>
<li><span class="add"><code>T</code>'s method has static parameters of its own.</span></li>
</ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The batch fixed walk's load check: walk now reads <code>self</code> of a self-typed trait as the checker does when it tests whether two domains exclude each other. So a program's own generic <code>+</code> or <code>-</code> between an array and a number now loads beside <code>AdditiveGroup</code>'s, as the library's pairs did already. The old text said that walk refuses such a pair in a program, which is no longer true, so a worker would have kept a test of a failure that is gone. The text now says that walk loads the pair, and names the two cases in which its load check still refuses it. It calls that check &ldquo;not the verdict&rdquo;, as the part's first section does, since the checker decides whether a pair is valid. After the cold read, the second case reads &ldquo;<code>T</code>'s method&rdquo;, since &ldquo;the method&rdquo; did not say whose.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l21" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 21 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Static parameters, where clauses and sizes&rdquo;</span>&ldquo;Accepts&rdquo; in place of &ldquo;compiles&rdquo;</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Static parameters, <code>where</code> clauses and sizes&rdquo;, line 70, its fourth sentence</p>
<p>The code generator <span class="cut">compiles</span> <span class="add">accepts</span> a declaration's own <code>where</code> clause <span class="cut">and</span> <span class="add">but</span> emits no code for it, so the compiled run time does not check it.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> &ldquo;Compiles&rdquo; reads as though code is generated for the clause, which the same sentence then denies. The code generator takes such a declaration without an error and generates nothing for the clause, so the sentence now says that it accepts the clause but emits no code for it.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l22" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 22 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Numbers&rdquo;</span>Tied declarations that differ at a numeral's position</h3>
