</section>

<section id="l24" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 24 &middot; revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Implementation limits&rdquo;</span>The fact and the plan in two sentences</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">revival-changes.md, &ldquo;Implementation limits&rdquo;, line 102, its bold rule and the sentence after it</p>
<p><strong>Walk has no static types<span class="cut"> until it runs the checker after the switch-over (P-12)</span>.</strong> <span class="add">Walk will run the checker only after the switch-over (P-12).</span> So walk runs programs that the checker refuses.</p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The bold rule tied a fact of today to a plan, and stated the plan as a fact. Elsewhere the skill describes the switch-over only as the compiled path's move onto the interpreter's library, so a reader new to the repository took &ldquo;until it runs the checker&rdquo; for a second meaning of the word, or a plan that the skill does not state. The bold rule now states only the fact, and the next sentence gives the plan, which is your decision P-12. The installed text wrote that sentence as &ldquo;It is to run the checker&rdquo;, which a worker could read as an order to itself. After the cold read it reads &ldquo;Walk will run the checker&rdquo;.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l25" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 25 &middot; interpreter.md, &ldquo;What walk checks&rdquo;</span>A new kind of pair checked by the overloading rules</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">interpreter.md, &ldquo;What walk checks&rdquo;, line 43, the third of the pairs that keep the team's check</p>
<ul><li>a pair that is neither two generic declarations<span class="add">,</span> nor a generic one beside a plain one<span class="cut">;</span><span class="add">, nor a generic function beside a generic trait's or object's functional method that has no static parameters of its own;</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The batch made walk check a new kind of pair by the overloading rules on declared domains: a generic function beside a functional method of a generic trait or object, where the method has no static parameters of its own. Walk now reads such a pair as two generic declarations. The list of the pairs that keep the team's old check still held it, so a worker would expect the team's message, not the name of the broken rule. The pair now leaves that list. The first wording, &ldquo;a functional method of a generic trait or object that has no static parameters of its own&rdquo;, let the last clause attach to the trait, which then could not be generic. After the cold read it reads &ldquo;a generic trait's or object's functional method that has&rdquo;.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l26" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 26 &middot; interpreter.md, &ldquo;What walk checks&rdquo;</span>A fixed refusal gone, and the departure the fix opened</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">interpreter.md, &ldquo;What walk checks&rdquo;, line 53, among walk's departures from the rules</p>
<ul><li><span class="cut">Where a program's own component declares both, it refuses a generic operator between an array of a number type and a number, on either side, beside the operator of a self-typed trait, as <code>AdditiveGroup</code>'s <code>+</code>: &ldquo;at least one pair of parameters must have excluding types&rdquo;. The checker accepts such a pair, and the library's own pairs load (ledger row 804).</span></li>
<li><span class="add">It does not refuse a type that extends a self-typed trait <code>T[\A\]</code> and is not an <code>A</code> (<code>revival-changes.md</code>, &ldquo;Traits and objects&rdquo;). A call that both <code>T</code>'s operator and another declaration fit then runs one of them, without an ambiguity error (ledger row 893). Make each type that extends <code>T[\A\]</code> an <code>A</code>.</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The first bullet said that walk refuses the self-typed pair in a program's own code. The batch fixed that, as change 20 says, so the bullet is gone. The fix opened a new departure, which the batch's skeptic found: walk now loads an object that extends a self-typed trait <code>T[\A\]</code> without being an <code>A</code>, which the specification makes a static error and the checker refuses. A call that two declarations fit, and neither more specifically, then runs one of them with no error. The new bullet says so. After the cold read, it adds that no ambiguity error comes, and what to do: make each such type an <code>A</code>, so that no worker relies on the declaration that walk happens to pick.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l27" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 27 &middot; interpreter.md, &ldquo;What walk checks&rdquo;</span>A function that walk loads and the checker refuses</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what was added</span>
<p class="where-in">interpreter.md, &ldquo;What walk checks&rdquo;, line 54, a new departure</p>
<ul><li><span class="add">It loads a generic top-level function whose declared domain lies inside that of a self-typed trait's functional method of the same name, where the Return Type Rule holds. The checker refuses every top-level function more specific than a functional method of its name, a check that the specification does not state: &lsquo;&hellip; is more specific than the declaration of the functional method&rsquo; (ledger row 894).</span></li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The skeptic found a second case where the paths now differ. Since the fix, walk loads a generic top-level function, such as a <code>+</code> over a subtype, whose domain lies inside that of a self-typed trait's operator of the same name, where the Return Type Rule holds. The checker refuses it, by a check that the specification does not state; the ledger marks the row as contested. The first wording, &ldquo;a generic function inside the declared domain&rdquo;, could be read as a function declared inside the method. It now says &ldquo;a top-level function whose declared domain lies inside that of&rdquo; the method. The cold read asked how a worker would recognise the checker's refusal, so the bullet now names the checker's check and quotes its message. The first wording also said that no decision yet says which path is right. That is a decision's status, which goes stale, so it was cut.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l28" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 28 &middot; interpreter.md, &ldquo;Natives&rdquo;</span>Which declared string types walk carries for a helper</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">interpreter.md, &ldquo;Natives&rdquo;, line 96, the second bullet of the Java types of a helper's binding</p>
<ul><li><span class="cut"><code>String</code>,</span> <code>FlatString</code> and <code>JavaString</code> in <code>String</code><span class="cut">;</span><span class="add">, and <code>String</code> too, except as a parameter other than <code>self</code>;</span> <code>ZZ</code> in <code>BigInteger</code>; <code>()</code> in <code>void</code>.</li></ul>
<p class="where-in">interpreter.md, &ldquo;Natives&rdquo;, line 100, the first bullet of the Java types of <code>self</code></p>
<ul><li>a type of the list above, except <span class="cut"><code>FlatString</code>,</span> <code>JavaString</code> and <code>()</code>, in the same Java type<span class="cut">;</span><span class="add">, but <code>FlatString</code> only where it is the library's own;</span></li></ul>
<p class="where-in">interpreter.md, &ldquo;Natives&rdquo;, line 108, the second of walk's refusals at load</p>
<ul><li>The result type, or a parameter type other than <code>self</code>'s, is not <span class="cut">in</span> <span class="add">carried by</span> the first list: &lsquo;no Java type carries the declared&rsquo;.</li></ul>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The batch changed which types walk carries for a helper's binding. A parameter declared <code>String</code>, other than <code>self</code>, is now refused at load, because a string that <code>||</code> builds would reach the native as the name of its type, not its text. A result or <code>self</code> of type <code>String</code> is still carried. And <code>self</code> of the library's own <code>FlatString</code> is now carried, so eleven of its methods bind helpers. The lists said that <code>String</code> is always carried, and <code>FlatString</code> never as <code>self</code>. They now say both. The refusal said &ldquo;not in the first list&rdquo;, but <code>String</code> is in the list and is still refused as a parameter, so it now reads &ldquo;not carried by the first list&rdquo;.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l29" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 29 &middot; interpreter.md, &ldquo;Natives&rdquo;</span>A program's own type named FlatString</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">interpreter.md, &ldquo;Natives&rdquo;, line 103</p>
<p>A program can give its own type the name <code>FlatString</code> or <code>JavaString</code><span class="cut">, so these names carry no <code>self</code>. So a binding in a method of the library's <code>FlatString</code> cannot name a helper yet: every program would stop at load.</span><span class="add">. Walk tells the library's <code>FlatString</code> by the component that declares it, so a program's own type of either name carries no <code>self</code>. But walk carries a parameter declared at such a type in <code>String</code>, by the type's name alone, and does not refuse the binding. The native then receives the name of the type, not the value's text (ledger row 877). Give a program's own type another name.</span></p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The old text said that a method of the library's <code>FlatString</code> cannot bind a helper, since every program would stop at load. That is no longer true: walk now tells the library's <code>FlatString</code> by the component that declares it, and eleven of its methods bind helpers. A program's own type of that name still carries no <code>self</code>. The skill writer added a trap that sits in the same place. A parameter declared at a program's own <code>FlatString</code> is still carried by the type's name, so the native gets the name of the type instead of the text, and nothing refuses it. After the cold read, the text says plainly that no refusal comes and that the text is wrong, and what to do: give the program's type another name.</p></div>
</section>

<section id="l30" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 30 &middot; interpreter.md, &ldquo;Natives&rdquo;</span>The natives that still get a CatString wrong, and the library's form</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, the words that changed; struck through, the words that went</span>
<p class="where-in">interpreter.md, &ldquo;Natives&rdquo;, line 117</p>
<p><span class="cut">A native declared over <code>String</code>, glue class or helper alike, receives</span> <span class="add">A glue class bound over <code>String</code>, and a helper bound in a method of <code>String</code> as <code>self</code>, receive</span> the wrong text for a <code>CatString</code><span class="cut">, as a parameter or as <code>self</code> in a method of <code>String</code></span>: the name of the type, not its characters (ledger row 846). <code>||</code> builds a <code>CatString</code> when the joined string is longer than <code>maxLeafSize</code>, 32 characters by default. <span class="cut">If a string that reaches such a native can come from <code>||</code>, pass it as <code>s.asFlatString</code> at the call of the native. In a method of <code>String</code>, call a function that binds the helper, with <code>self.asFlatString</code>.</span> <span class="add">Bind such a native over <code>FlatString</code>, and beside it an overload over <code>String</code> that passes <code>s.asFlatString</code>, as <code>printlnWithThread</code> does (<code>Library/FortressLibrary.fss:4640-4644</code>). In a method of <code>String</code>, call that overload with <code>self</code>.</span></p>
