<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> Since a helper's <code>String</code> parameter is now refused at load (change 28), a helper can no longer receive a <code>CatString</code> as a parameter. Two cases still get the wrong text: a glue class bound over <code>String</code>, and a helper bound as <code>self</code> in a method of <code>String</code>. The old text named every native over <code>String</code> alike. Its advice was to convert the string at each call of the native, which every caller must remember. The text now gives the library's own form: the native over <code>FlatString</code>, and an overload over <code>String</code> that converts once. The first rewrite dropped the advice for a method of <code>String</code>; after the cold read, the text says to call that overload with <code>self</code>.</p></div>
</section>

