<section id="l32" class="part item">
<h3><span class="num">Change 32 &middot; compiler.md, &ldquo;Natives&rdquo;</span>A helper class with a type that only the compiler's prelude has</h3>
<div class="change solo"><div><span class="side">New text; underlined, what was added</span>
<p class="where-in">compiler.md, &ldquo;Natives&rdquo;, line 96, the paragraph on the wrapper generator</p>
<p>The wrapper generator wraps every method of a class that it is given, the private methods too. If a type has no Fortress counterpart, it fails the whole import. So give every method of a helper class a signature that maps to Fortress, also a private method. <span class="add">In the count stage and the distance stage, a type maps only if the interpreter's library declares it. So there, a binding stops its component if any method of its helper's class has a type that only the compiler's prelude declares. The distance stage then counts the component as a stopped target. <code>nativeHelpers/systemOps</code> is such a class: its <code>getArgs</code> answers <code>StringVector</code>, so the message is &lsquo;FortressBuiltin.StringVector is undefined.&rsquo; (ledger row 895). Bind a helper of another class.</span></p>
</div></div>
<div class="note"><p><strong>Why.</strong> The paragraph said that every method of a helper class must map to Fortress, but not that, in the two stages that check the interpreter's library, it is that library which decides what maps. The batch found the case: one method of <code>systemOps</code> answers <code>StringVector</code>, a type that only the compiler's prelude declares, so a binding of any helper of that class stops its whole component. The first wording named only that class, without the reason, so a worker could bind a helper of another class with the same flaw. After the cold read, the text gives the general rule, why <code>systemOps</code> falls under it, what the distance stage then counts, and what to do: bind a helper of another class.</p></div>
</section>

